  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sacaton Flats Village’s O’odham name, Ha:shañ Ke:k, literally means “town‑like area” and signals its status as a community without its own government.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://kids.kiddle.co/Sacaton_Flats_Village,_Arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Located at roughly 33°03′21″N 111°39′32″W, Sacaton Flats Village sits in Pinal County’s Gila River Indian Community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sacaton_flats_village_arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Summer highs in Sacaton Flats routinely exceed 105°F, while winter nights can drop to about 40°F.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sacaton_flats_village_arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After the Hohokam fell, the Pima revived flood‑water farming on the Gila, building on ancient canal techniques.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sacaton_flats_village_arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "President Buchanan’s 1859 executive order created the Gila River Reservation, the first reservation in Arizona, encompassing Sacaton Flats.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sacaton_flats_village_arizona"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Upstream diversions have left the Gila River largely dry through the community, forcing reliance on groundwater and imported water.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
